                "notes": "### 📌 Architectural Overview & Core Invariants\n- **Monotonic Window Boundary Invariant**: In two-pointer and sliding window algorithms, both the left pointer $L$ and right pointer $R$ advance strictly monotonically forward, yielding strict linear $O(N)$ amortized runtime.\n- **Shrinking Condition**: The right pointer $R$ expands the window to include new elements; whenever the problem constraint is violated, the left pointer $L$ advances until the invariant is restored.\n\n### ⚙️ Mechanics & Complexity Bounds\n- **Amortized Analysis**: Even with a nested `while` loop shrinking the window, each element enters the window once ($R$) and leaves at most once ($L$). Total operations: $\\le 2N \\implies O(N)$ time, $O(K)$ space for frequency maps.\n\n### 💻 Production Implementation Pattern\n```python\ndef length_of_longest_substring_k_distinct(s: str, k: int) -> int:\n    char_count = {}\n    left = 0\n    max_len = 0\n\n    for right, ch in enumerate(s):\n        char_count[ch] = char_count.get(ch, 0) + 1\n\n        # Shrink window until distinct count <= k\n        while len(char_count) > k:\n            left_ch = s[left]\n            char_count[left_ch] -= 1\n            if char_count[left_ch] == 0:\n                del char_count[left_ch]\n            left += 1\n\n        max_len = max(max_len, right - left + 1)\n\n    return max_len\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Interview Traps & Failure Modes\n- **Off-By-One Window Length**: The length of the inclusive window $[L, R]$ is always $R - L + 1$, not $R - L$.\n- **Negative Numbers in Sliding Window**: Dynamic sliding window sum algorithms fail if array contains negative numbers because the window sum is no longer monotonic. In that case, use **Prefix Sum + Hash Map**.\n\n### 🎯 FAANG Key Takeaways\n- For sliding window maximum/minimum in $O(1)$ time per slide, utilize a **Monotonic Decreasing Deque** storing indices."
