                "notes": "### 📌 Architectural Overview & Core Invariants\n- **Cooperative Multitasking Invariant**: Python `asyncio` operates on a single-threaded event loop utilizing OS-level I/O multiplexing (`epoll` on Linux, `kqueue` on macOS).\n- **The Global Interpreter Lock (GIL)**: A CPython mutex preventing simultaneous multi-threaded bytecode execution. Use `multiprocessing` or C-extensions for CPU-bound tasks, and `asyncio` for high-concurrency I/O workloads.\n\n### ⚙️ Mechanics & Memory Profiles\n- **Generators & Iterators ($O(1)$ Memory)**: Generator functions maintain frame state and evaluate lazily using `yield`, streaming millions of records without loading entire datasets into memory.\n- **Task Scheduling**: Coroutines yield control to the event loop explicitly at `await` expressions.\n\n### 💻 Production Implementation Pattern\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom typing import AsyncGenerator\n\nasync def fetch_batch(batch_id: int) -> dict:\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.05) # Non-blocking I/O simulation\n    return {\"batch\": batch_id, \"status\": \"ok\"}\n\nasync def stream_pipeline(total_batches: int) -> AsyncGenerator[dict, None]:\n    async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as tg:\n        tasks = [tg.create_task(fetch_batch(i)) for i in range(total_batches)]\n    for t in tasks:\n        yield t.result()\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Interview Traps & Failure Modes\n- **Blocking the Event Loop**: Calling synchronous blocking functions (e.g. `time.sleep()`, `requests.get()`, or heavy math) inside a coroutine halts all other concurrent tasks in the loop. Always use `asyncio.to_thread()`.\n- **Unhandled Exceptions in Fire-and-Forget Tasks**: Creating tasks without saving references or awaiting them causes silent exception loss and garbage collection warnings.\n\n### 🎯 FAANG Key Takeaways\n- In Python 3.11+, always prefer `asyncio.TaskGroup()` structured concurrency over legacy `asyncio.gather()` for strict exception propagation."
