                "notes": "### 📌 Architectural Overview & Core Invariants\n- **Linux Namespaces & Cgroups**: Containers are isolated Linux processes governed by PID, NET, MNT, IPC, and UTS namespaces, with CPU and memory limits enforced by Control Groups (cgroups v2).\n- **Multi-Stage Build Invariant**: Production images must strictly contain runtime dependencies and compiled binaries, completely discarding compilers, build tools, package managers, and source code.\n\n### ⚙️ Mechanics & Layer Caching\n- **Union File System (Overlay2)**: Each Dockerfile command creates an immutable read-only layer. Ordering commands from least frequently changed (`COPY package.json`) to most frequently changed (`COPY src/`) maximizes build cache utilization.\n\n### 💻 Production Implementation Pattern\n```dockerfile\n# Stage 1: Build & Compile\nFROM golang:1.23-alpine AS builder\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY go.mod go.sum ./\nRUN go mod download\nCOPY . .\nRUN CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux go build -ldflags=\"-w -s\" -o server .\n\n# Stage 2: Distroless Minimal Runtime\nFROM gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot\nWORKDIR /\nCOPY --from=builder /app/server /server\nUSER nonroot:nonroot\nEXPOSE 8080\nENTRYPOINT [\"/server\"]\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Interview Traps & Failure Modes\n- **Running as Root (UID 0)**: If an attacker breaks out of a root container via a kernel vulnerability (e.g. Dirty COW or runc CVE), they obtain host root privileges. Always declare `USER nonroot` (UID > 10000).\n- **Zombie Process Leaks**: PID 1 in a container must handle `SIGCHLD` signals to reap terminated child processes. Use an init process like `tini` for complex multi-process containers.\n\n### 🎯 FAANG Key Takeaways\n- Minimize attack surface by using distroless or scratch base images, bringing vulnerability scan alerts (CVEs) down to 0."
