                "notes": "### 📌 Architectural Overview & Core Invariants\n- **CAP Theorem Invariant**: In any distributed network with unavoidable network partitions ($P$), a system must choose between Linearizable Consistency ($C$) or Total Availability ($A$).\n- **Consistent Hashing**: Maps nodes and keys onto a $2^{32}-1$ hash ring with virtual nodes (tokens), ensuring that adding or removing a node migrates only $K/N$ keys on average.\n\n### ⚙️ Mechanics & Consensus Quorums\n- **Strict Quorum Equation**: For $N$ total replicas, read quorum $R$ and write quorum $W$:\n  \\[ R + W > N \\]\n  Guarantees that at least one replica in the read set contains the most recent monotonic write timestamp.\n\n### 💻 Production Implementation Pattern\n```python\nimport hashlib\nimport bisect\n\nclass ConsistentHashRing:\n    def __init__(self, replicas: int = 150):\n        self.replicas = replicas\n        self.ring = [] # Sorted hash keys\n        self.node_map = {} # hash -> node_id\n\n    def add_node(self, node_id: str):\n        for i in range(self.replicas):\n            h = int(hashlib.md5(f\"{node_id}:{i}\".encode()).hexdigest(), 16)\n            bisect.insort(self.ring, h)\n            self.node_map[h] = node_id\n\n    def get_node(self, key: str) -> str:\n        if not self.ring: return \"\"\n        h = int(hashlib.md5(key.encode()).hexdigest(), 16)\n        idx = bisect.bisect_right(self.ring, h) % len(self.ring)\n        return self.node_map[self.ring[idx]]\n```\n\n### ⚠️ Common Interview Traps & Failure Modes\n- **Split-Brain Scenarios**: Network partitions splitting cluster nodes can cause two sub-clusters to elect independent leaders, causing irreversible data divergence. Enforce odd node counts ($2F+1$) and majority quorums.\n- **Thundering Herd / Cache Stampede**: When a high-traffic cache key expires, thousands of concurrent requests hit the database simultaneously. Use distributed mutex locks or Probabilistic Early Expiration (XFetch).\n\n### 🎯 FAANG Key Takeaways\n- Always establish Non-Functional Requirements (NFRs) upfront: Daily Active Users, Read/Write QPS ratio, p99 Latency SLA (<50ms), and 5-Year Storage footprint."
